use ../../lib/UiBehavior

export ui-behavior FormParticipation:
    description:
        FormParticipation lets a control's value take part in a surrounding
        form, just as the platform's own form fields do. It belongs to the
        control, and matters most for controls that are built from scratch
        rather than being a form field underneath, such as a Slider or a
        Select.

    name:
        The control can be given a name. With a name, its value is sent with
        the form under that name. Without one, the control takes no part in
        sending the form.

    submission:
        The value is sent as text, just as the platform's own field of the
        same kind would send it. A control holding several values, such as a
        range with two ends, sends each under the same name. A control with no
        value sends nothing, as an unticked checkbox sends nothing. How a value
        with no obvious text form, such as a colour or a drawing, is turned
        into text is up to the chosen library.

    form:
        The control belongs to the form it sits inside. It can also be tied to
        a form elsewhere on the page.

    reset:
        When the form is reset, the control goes back to the value it started
        with, which is not necessarily empty, and the component using it is
        told.

    required:
        A control can be required, and assistive tools are told that it is.
        Whether sending the form with a required control left empty is
        stopped by the platform or checked by the form is up to the chosen
        library.

    invalid:
        The component or form can mark the control as invalid, usually after
        checking it. The control shows that it is invalid, and assistive tools
        are told, along with any error message that goes with it.

    disabled:
        A disabled control can't be changed and its value isn't sent. A
        control is also disabled when a group of fields around it is disabled
        as a whole.

    readOnly:
        A read-only control can't be changed, but can still be focused and
        copied from, and its value is still sent.

    autofill:
        Whether the platform can fill the control in for the user, such as with
        a saved address, is up to the chosen library.

    nonGoals:
        - It doesn't decide what's valid, or when to show errors. That belongs
            to the form
        - It doesn't send the form. It only makes the value available when the
            form is sent
